# Copy to .env. Every setting is OTO_<SECTION>_<KEY>; see internal/platform/config.
# Everything here is a default that already works against `make db-up`.

OTO_ENV=dev
OTO_SERVICE=oto

# --- HTTP ---
OTO_HTTP_ADDR=:8080
OTO_HTTP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080
# Comma-separated, and it DEFAULTS TO EMPTY (CORS disabled). Every origin listed
# here can read authenticated responses with the session cookie attached, so a
# production deployment names its real UI origin and nothing else. `*` is refused
# at boot: oto sends credentials, and no browser honours a wildcard with those.
# The Vite dev server proxies instead, so this is only a fallback for dev.
OTO_HTTP_CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173

# --- Database (matches docker-compose.yml) ---
OTO_DB_URL=postgres://oto:oto@localhost:5432/oto?sslmode=disable
OTO_DB_MAX_CONNS=20
# SPEC §G.10: the ingest pool is carved out so UI queries can never starve ingestion.
OTO_DB_INGEST_SHARE_PERCENT=25
OTO_DB_INGEST_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT=2s
OTO_DB_GENERAL_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT=15s

# --- Logging ---
OTO_LOG_LEVEL=info
OTO_LOG_FORMAT=text
# Redaction is PER SOURCE, not per process: set `redact_labels` / `redact_annotations`
# on the alert source (POST/PATCH /api/v1/sources). They are glob patterns applied to
# label and annotation VALUES before `ingest_batches.payload` is written, so a matched
# value never reaches disk. There is no OTO_LOG_REDACT_LABELS; there was, and it
# redacted nothing.

# --- Telemetry ---
OTO_TELEMETRY_METRICS_ENABLED=true
OTO_TELEMETRY_TRACING_ENABLED=false
OTO_TELEMETRY_OTLP_ENDPOINT=localhost:4317

# --- Ingest (SPEC §C.9, §G.10) ---
# The B1-B17 payload bounds are NOT configurable: 8 MiB body, 10000 alerts per batch,
# 64 labels per alert. Each is bound to a DDL CHECK or to the domain LabelSet
# constructor, so an environment variable that disagreed with it would turn a rejected
# alert into a 500. See internal/ingestion/domain/bounds.go for every value.
#
# Retry-After sent with a 503, and how long a webhook may wait for an ingest slot
# before oto sheds it. Both are honoured.
OTO_INGEST_RETRY_AFTER=10s
OTO_INGEST_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT=500ms

# --- Retention ---
# ⛔ THE ONLY TWO SETTINGS IN OTO THAT DELETE ANYTHING. Partitions are dropped
# whole and there is no undo and no export (ADR 0024, docs/setup/tuning.md).
# These are a FLOOR: partitions.manage drops at the maximum of these and every
# org's own orgs.settings, because a partition holds every tenant's rows.
#
# 720h = 30 days of raw webhook bodies. Chosen, not derived: a replay is refused
# when the alerts a batch would touch have moved on since it arrived, never because
# the batch is old (ADR 0024, Amendment 4), so this window is the depth of the
# rejections and failed-batch feeds — which take no date range — and the window in
# which a stored batch can be replayed at all. No alert page is served from here.
OTO_RETENTION_RAW_PAYLOADS=720h
# 9360h = 13 months of the instant-by-instant timeline. Dropping a month destroys
# every human comment and unack note in it — those live nowhere else. The alert,
# its episodes, the acks, the rule text and the delivery record are never reaped.
OTO_RETENTION_EVENTS=9360h

# --- Slack ---
# Interactivity — the Acknowledge button on every alert card — needs mode=http, a
# signing secret, and the Slack app's Request URL pointed at
# POST /api/v1/integrations/slack/interactions. SOCKET MODE IS NOT IMPLEMENTED:
# `socket` is still the default and disables the endpoint without enabling
# anything in its place, so a deployment left here renders a button that nothing
# is listening for. See docs/setup/slack.md.
OTO_SLACK_ENABLED=false
OTO_SLACK_MODE=socket
# MUST be non-empty in http mode: an empty signing secret accepts forged requests,
# and it is the ONLY thing authenticating the interactions endpoint.
OTO_SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=

# --- Secrets and deployment-level trust ---
# base64 of 32 random bytes: openssl rand -base64 32
OTO_SECURITY_SECRET_KEY=

# SSRF guard. DEFAULT CLOSED. Turn it on ONLY on a single-tenant, self-hosted
# install whose Alertmanager/Prometheus/webhook receiver really is on a private
# network -- with it on, oto will dial 10.0.0.0/8, 127.0.0.1 and 169.254.169.254
# on behalf of any tenant that configures a source pointing there.
OTO_SECURITY_ALLOW_PRIVATE_TARGETS=false

# Lets alert_sources.tls_skip_verify take effect. DEFAULT CLOSED: that column is
# tenant-writable, and whether an unverified certificate is acceptable is a
# decision about YOUR network, not about one org's source.
OTO_SECURITY_ALLOW_INSECURE_TLS=false

# Login limits. argon2id costs 19 MiB per verification and runs on every path
# (including unknown addresses, deliberately), so these bound both brute force
# and memory: burst per client address, one token back per refill interval, and a
# hard cap on concurrent verifications.
OTO_SECURITY_LOGIN_RATE_BURST=5
OTO_SECURITY_LOGIN_RATE_REFILL=12s
OTO_SECURITY_LOGIN_MAX_CONCURRENT=8

# --- Local dev Alertmanager wiring (compose only, read by `just am-wire`) ---
#
# NOT read by oto. These three exist because the compose Alertmanager has to be
# told where to POST and with what token, and Alertmanager expands NO
# environment variables -- a literal `$VAR` in its config is parsed as a URL and
# fails at load. So `just am-wire` reads these and renders
# deploy/alertmanager/local/{webhook_url,ingest_token}, which the receiver picks
# up via `url_file` / `credentials_file`. It runs automatically before `just infra`.
#
# Both values come from creating a source; the token is returned exactly once.
# Leave them unset and the receiver points at the all-zero uuid, which 404s
# loudly rather than posting to a route that does not exist.
#
# The URL is written from oto's point of view; am-wire rewrites localhost to
# host.docker.internal, because Alertmanager dials it from inside a container.
OTO_AM_LOCAL_WEBHOOK=
OTO_AM_LOCAL_INGEST_TOKEN=
# A PAT for poking the API by hand (`just stream`, curl). Also not read by oto.
OTO_AM_LOCAL_PAT=

# --- Declarative tuning (docs/setup/tuning.md) ---
#
# EVERYTHING HERE IS COMMENTED OUT ON PURPOSE. Precedence is
#
#   shipped default  ->  org override (Postgres)  ->  these  (highest)
#
# so uncommenting a line TAKES THE SETTING AWAY FROM THE UI: the settings API
# reports its origin as `config`, names the variable below so an operator knows
# where to change it, and refuses a PATCH on that key with 409 rather than
# accepting a value that would revert on your next deploy. An override the org
# already wrote is kept and reported as `shadowed`, and takes effect again the
# moment you delete the line here.
#
# Use these when oto is deployed from Helm values or a config file and the
# repository is meant to be the truth. Leave them alone otherwise and let each org
# tune itself. Every key on docs/setup/tuning.md works here; the bounds are the
# same ones the API enforces, and a bad value fails the BOOT rather than being
# quietly ignored.
#
# ⛔ `refire_grace_s` and `group_close_delay_s` were advertised here and are GONE
# (git-bug 7287b28). A line naming either fails the boot with `unknown_key` rather
# than being ignored, so they are removed instead of left commented out: a commented
# example is documentation, and documenting a key that cannot boot is worse than
# documenting nothing.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND SO WERE FIVE MORE, ON THE SAME RULE, AND THEY OUTLIVED THE KEYS THEY
# NAMED BY LONGER. `unacked_reminder_after_s` and the three
# `unacked_reminder_mention*` keys went with the unacked reminder (git-bug bd0fb1d,
# migration 00068) — the owner withdrew the feature, oto sends nothing unprompted —
# and `broadcast_on_resolved` went with Slack thread-broadcast (git-bug 7570090):
# with no `reply_broadcast` call left in the notifier there was no broadcast for an
# org to opt into. All five stayed commented out down here long after their keys
# stopped existing, which is the exact failure mode this tombstone is
# written to stop recurring: `identity/domain.NewDeclarative` refuses an unknown key
# AT BOOT, so somebody uncommenting a line an example file offered them got a server
# that would not start — and the example file was where they went to find out which
# lines were safe. A commented key is a promise, and the seven below are the only
# ones oto can keep.
#
# ⚠️ THE SEVEN BELOW ARE THE WHOLE KEY SET, and they are exactly
# `identity/domain.AllSettingKeys()` — that list is the authority, this one is a copy
# for an operator's eyes. Adding a line here that is not in it does not add a
# setting; it adds a boot failure.
# OTO_TUNING_RESOLVE_GRACE_S=300
# OTO_TUNING_FLAP_THRESHOLD=5
# OTO_TUNING_FLAP_WINDOW_S=10800
# OTO_TUNING_FLAP_DIGEST_INTERVAL_S=900
# OTO_TUNING_RAW_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# OTO_TUNING_EVENT_RETENTION_MONTHS=13
# OTO_TUNING_DEFAULT_VERBOSITY=status_changes
